on february 11th 2013  pope benedict xvi in front of the  college of cardinals  and in front of the world retired as  pope  he cited his age and lack of strength of  mind and body as the reasons for his  resignation  his retirement took place on the 28th of  the same month  and he was succeeded by the current pope  pope  francis it had been almost 600 years  since the last pope had renounced  the papacy but this video is not about  this pope benedict  but a different pope benedict pope  benedict the ninth  the first pope to willfully renounce the  papacy  before we begin it is important to point  out that through the history of the  papacy  99 of popes held the title till their  death  before benedict ix there had been a  number of popes  who lost their position before dying  these men did not do so willingly  however  they were usually driven off into exile  in other words they were deposed this  is not the case with benedict the night  born theophylactus of tusculum the  future pope was of noble blood  his father was a feudal lord the count  of tusculum  this position was one of the most  powerful in medieval italy  and especially in the city of rome he  was also the nephew of two previous  popes  pope benedict the viii and john xiv  keep these two facts in mind not  much is known about his early childhood  but when he was a young man his father  bribed the officials of rome to make the  aphalactus  pope some sources say that he may have  been  as young as 11 or 12 but most historians  agree he was about 20.  you may be wondering how this happened  you can't just bribe some random public  officials to become  pope right you have to be elected by the  college of cardinals  we've all seen the cgb grave video after  all  well before 1059 there was no uniform  way to elect a pope  officially the pope was chosen by a  college of bishops in the area in  and around rome but this election was  often a rubber  stamp of sorts for the selected  candidate it was usual for instance for  popes to choose their own successor or  for important kings and emperors to put  their preferred man in place  at this time the officials and nobles of  rome held that power  and so they were bribed by the count of  tusculum to put his son  in this position making him pope  benedict the ninth  in 1032 the papacy at  this time was as much a political  position as it was a spiritual one  the pope was the monarch of the papal  states as well as the leader of  all of western christendom and this was  even before the great schism  when the eastern orthodox church broke  from the catholic church  so the pope had considerable sway in the  east as well  a family who had a pope on the throne  was a powerful family indeed  and as was previously noted benedict had  two uncles who were pope before him  and while it's not unheard of for  families to have political dynasties in  democratic systems  in this instance it was not so innocent  in catholic history this period is known  as the tusculum papacy in which  three successive popes were put on the  papal chair  because of the meddling of the family of  tusculum so  already we're off to a bad start but  let's not judge old benedict too  quickly just because his father bought  him the papacy  doesn't mean he'll be a bad pope right  wrong  benedict ix papacy was racked with  controversy  he was accused of a great number of  atrocities from  rampant corruption and bribery and even  murder and rape  theologians and historians have called  him a demon from hell  and a disgrace to the chair of saint  peter in truth  we don't know how accurate these claims  are in the 10th and 11th centuries  the church was extremely fractured and  corrupt many of these accusations  may have well been overblown by the  political rivals to benedict and his  family  still it is safe to say he was a  negligent pope at the very  least as nothing of significance is  attributed to his reign  his papacy was so controversial that in  1036 he was  run out of rome and forced into exile by  city officials  he came back quickly however with the  help of the holy roman emperor of the  time  conrad ii but only a few years later  in 1044 opposition to benedict's  lifestyle again led to his banishment  from the city  this time the church actually replaced  him electing  sylvester iii who was a member of the  crescensi family  the main rivals of the tusculums in  september of that year  but again he came back while in exile  benedict gathered up an army and marched  into rome  deposed sylvester and was back on the  papal throne  that's a lot of excitement already and  we haven't even gotten to the point of  the video  after taking back the papal seat  benedict started to become  uneasy he wasn't certain that he could  hold on to the papacy  and on top of that it seemed he was  rather over the whole thing  he expressed interest in mary and his  cousin and since  marriage was obviously not allowed for a  pope benedict ix  decided to abdicate he went to his  godfather  the priest john gracian and consulted  him on his decision  john agreed that benedict's leaving  would be in the best interest of the  church  and so benedict offered john the  position if john would reimburse  benedict for some of his expenses  essentially benedict sold the papacy  to his godfather john gracian accepted  the offer  paid the money and was elected as pope  gregory vi  contrary to what you might think pope  gregory was seen both by  his contemporaries and by historians as  a pretty good choice for pope  he was well respected and really cared  about seeding out the corruption in the  church  he was ultimately unsuccessful on that  front  but a for effort so that's the story of  the first  willful abdication wait what the story  isn't over  benedict apparently growing bored of his  new life and  new wife came to regret his decision to  abdicate and  sell the papacy so what did he do well  gather another army and storm the city  of rome of course asserting his claim to  the papacy for the  third time in his life he was successful  in taking the city  but none of the clergy nor the laity  recognized him as pope  instead sticking with gregory hearing  about aldous kerfuffle  sylvester iii remember him decided this  would be a great time to reassert  his claim to the position as well so by  the year 1046  there were three count em three  claimants  to the papacy quite the mess  over in the holy roman empire emperor  henry iii was hearing rumors of chaos  down in rome the place he was  supposedly emperor of a number of  italian bishops  and laypeople had sent messages urging  him to march down the rome  and bring order to the city and so he  did  in december of 1046 the council of  suttree was called to try and fix the  mess  and hash out who would be the next pope  it was decided that none of the three  claims to the papacy were valid  and a new pope would be chosen pope  clement ii  benedict actually didn't show up to the  council at all  and refused to accept the council's  rulings  when clement ii died he tried to take  the papacy again  but was expelled by german forces he was  excommunicated soon after  it is rumored that he later regretted  the whole affair and saw the error in  his ways  pope leo ix lifted his excommunication  and benedict died in 1056 being buried  at the abbey of gratiferata  so that's the story of pope benedict ix  the first pope to willfully step  down as pope and then reserve his claim  as pope and then refused to step down  when he was asked to  hopefully that gives you a good view  into the chaos of the early papacy  and the convoluted and corrupt politics  of the middle ages  we can take comfort in that today's  papal renunciations  are done quite a bit more peacefully  thanks for watching
 

